# Project rules

This is a small production website for one independent practice in India. It is not a SaaS product. Public marketing pages and a paid booking flow are in scope. An admin dashboard, user accounts, a CMS, analytics, and two-way calendar sync are out of scope.

## Product and code rules

1. Do not add features that were not requested. Do not create an admin dashboard.
2. Keep the architecture appropriate for a small production website. Avoid unnecessary dependencies. Before installing one, write a one-line justification.
3. All secrets, payments, OAuth, and sensitive operations stay on the server. Never expose the Razorpay secret, Google client secret, Google refresh token, Supabase service-role key, database credentials, or email API keys to the browser. Only `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables may reach the client, and none of them may be secret.
4. Use environment variables for all credentials. Validate them with a typed env module.
5. Validate all input on the server with zod, even when the client already validated it.
6. Never trust a frontend payment status. Verify Razorpay signatures on the server. Never write fake or mock payment-success logic on a production code path.
7. Never log secrets or personal data (email, phone, name, tokens, signatures).
8. Business logic lives in `src/lib` and stays framework-independent where practical. UI components stay thin.
9. The site is fully responsive (mobile first) and accessible: semantic HTML, labels, focus states, keyboard navigation, sufficient contrast, and reduced motion respected. It is also SEO-friendly.
10. Placeholder or dummy content must be clearly marked and must never be presented as a real claim. Do not invent qualifications, testimonials, statistics, or professional claims.
11. After every meaningful change run `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`, and `npm run build`. Fix errors before moving on.
12. Explain any large architectural change in `docs/DECISIONS.md` before implementing it.
13. Store money as integer paise, never floats. Store timestamps as UTC `timestamptz`. Display them in the business timezone (default `Asia/Kolkata`, configurable).
14. Minimize sensitive data. Collect only name, email, phone, and an optional short note. Never ask for clinical or health details.

## Autonomy and safety rules

The user is not watching this run.

15. Never ask the user questions and never wait for confirmation. If something is ambiguous, pick the safest reasonable option and append an entry to `docs/DECISIONS.md` (date, decision, reason, alternative considered). Stop early only for something truly blocking: a missing credential that cannot be stubbed, or an irreversible action.
16. Git: after each completed and verified phase, make one commit with a clear message. Never push. Never force anything. Never run `git reset --hard`, `git clean`, `git rebase`, or anything else that rewrites history. Stay on the current branch.
17. Never deploy and never run `vercel`, `supabase db push`, `supabase db reset`, or any command that touches a remote or production service. Use only test or dev credentials. If a command would affect anything outside this folder, do not run it, and note it in `docs/DECISIONS.md` instead.
18. Never run destructive commands (`rm -rf` outside build artefacts such as `.next` or `node_modules`, deleting files outside the project, `sudo`). Never edit files outside the project folder. Never print or commit the contents of `.env` files.
19. Never run interactive commands that wait for input. Always pass non-interactive flags. Start long-running processes such as the dev server in the background with a time limit, test them with curl, and always kill them afterward. Never leave a process running and never block on one.
20. Fix-loop limit: if the same error persists after three genuinely different fix attempts, stop trying. If the attempts made things worse, revert that specific change by editing, not with `git reset`. Record the problem in `docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md` with the error text and what was tried, then continue with the remaining work.
